      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="portfolio-grid case-study-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Project Overview</span>
              <h2>What the system does and why teams depended on it</h2>
              <p>ProfOutlet is an e-commerce platform built for retail businesses and merchants managing expanding digital catalogs. The product was designed to give merchants a modern storefront and operations layer instead of a fragile, admin-heavy commerce setup.</p>
              <p>From an engineering perspective, the system had to handle growing catalogs, campaign-driven traffic, checkout sessions, and order-state changes without creating operational slowdowns. That made it a production platform with real operational demands rather than a portfolio demo.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Business Challenge</span>
              <h2>What had to improve for the product to scale cleanly</h2>
              <p>The core business challenge was delivering a product that could serve e-commerce and online retail use cases while still remaining easy for operators to trust and use every day.</p>
              <p>The business needed stronger catalog control, cleaner checkout flows, and a maintainable base for revenue-focused merchandising improvements.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Constraints &amp; Complexity</span>
              <h3>Engineering realities that shaped implementation choices</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The delivery had to solve product problems, operational risk, and scaling concerns at the same time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expertise-grid project-complexity-grid">
            
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Catalog Depth</h3>
              <p>Large product sets and nested categories needed to remain easy to manage and fast to browse.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Order Lifecycle</h3>
              <p>Checkout, payment state, and fulfillment actions had to stay traceable for support teams.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Merchant Velocity</h3>
              <p>Publishing and editing products could not depend on developer support for daily operations.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Conversion Focus</h3>
              <p>Storefront UX had to support business growth, not just backend correctness.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-accent">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Solution Architecture</span>
              <h2>System design, integrations, and delivery approach</h2>
            </div>
            <p>The implementation emphasized maintainability, production-readiness, and cleaner operational workflows.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="case-study-hero architecture-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <h3>Implementation approach</h3>
              <p>The solution used Laravel 11, Bagisto, Vue 3, Tailwind CSS, MySQL across a modular application architecture. Core workflows were separated into clearer domain boundaries so business logic, integrations, and UI concerns did not become tightly coupled.</p>
              <p>That approach made the system easier to scale, easier to monitor, and easier to extend as new requirements appeared. It also created better release confidence for a product expected to handle real customer traffic and operator activity.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel architecture-diagram-panel">
              <span class="eyebrow">Architecture Diagram</span>
              <div class="architecture-diagram" aria-label="System architecture">
                <div class="arch-node">Storefront UI</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">Commerce Services</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-row">
                  <div class="arch-node small">Checkout</div>
                  <div class="arch-node small">Merchant Admin</div>
                </div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">MySQL Orders</div>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Key Features</span>
              <h3>Product capabilities designed for business outcomes</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Every capability was selected because it made the platform more useful, more scalable, or easier to operate.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="services-grid">
            
            <article class="service-card">
              <i class="bi bi-grid"></i>
              <h3>Catalog & Variant Management</h3>
              <p>Organized products, collections, and variants into scalable admin workflows.</p>
              <span>Made ongoing merchandising work faster and less error-prone.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="service-card">
              <i class="bi bi-bag-check"></i>
              <h3>Checkout Optimization</h3>
              <p>Improved cart, shipping, and payment interactions across the conversion funnel.</p>
              <span>Helped reduce friction before purchase completion.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="service-card">
              <i class="bi bi-box-seam"></i>
              <h3>Inventory Visibility</h3>
              <p>Tracked stock-sensitive products and order availability inside one operational surface.</p>
              <span>Reduced oversell risk and support confusion.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="service-card">
              <i class="bi bi-receipt-cutoff"></i>
              <h3>Order Administration</h3>
              <p>Provided clear status tracking, order actions, and merchant oversight for post-purchase workflows.</p>
              <span>Improved internal fulfillment coordination.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="service-card">
              <i class="bi bi-bar-chart-line"></i>
              <h3>Promotion Readiness</h3>
              <p>Prepared the platform for campaign-driven merchandising and category visibility improvements.</p>
              <span>Made it easier to run revenue-focused storefront changes.</span>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-deep">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Performance &amp; Scalability</span>
              <h2>Technical decisions that improved stability and maintainability</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Architecture choices focused on safe growth, cleaner releases, and operational clarity.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expertise-grid">
            
            <article class="feature-card">
              <h3>Asynchronous workflow handling</h3>
              <p><strong>Why chosen:</strong> The platform needed to support handle growing catalogs, campaign-driven traffic, checkout sessions, and order-state changes without creating operational slowdowns without blocking the main user journey.</p>
              <p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> This added queue, retry, and observability responsibilities to the architecture.</p>
              <span class="feature-meta"><strong>Outcome:</strong> Heavy tasks moved out of request-response flows, keeping the product faster and more stable.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <h3>Service-layer integration boundaries</h3>
              <p><strong>Why chosen:</strong> The product relied on multiple external or subsystem workflows across e-commerce and online retail.</p>
              <p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> More explicit service boundaries required extra design effort early in delivery.</p>
              <span class="feature-meta"><strong>Outcome:</strong> The codebase became safer to extend and easier to debug as the platform evolved.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <h3>Domain-first product modeling</h3>
              <p><strong>Why chosen:</strong> The business needed stronger operational clarity around give merchants a modern storefront and operations layer instead of a fragile, admin-heavy commerce setup.</p>
              <p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> It took longer to model states and rules than a quick CRUD implementation would have.</p>
              <span class="feature-meta"><strong>Outcome:</strong> The result was a system with clearer workflows, better reporting, and lower long-term maintenance risk.</span>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Technologies Used</span>
              <h3>Production stack behind the platform</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Chosen to support maintainable delivery, good product performance, and cleaner operations.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stack-grid project-stack-minimal">
            <article class="stack-group"><h3>Frontend</h3><p>Vue 3, Tailwind CSS, Blade views</p></article><article class="stack-group"><h3>Backend</h3><p>Laravel 11, Bagisto modules</p></article><article class="stack-group"><h3>Data</h3><p>MySQL product, order, and inventory data</p></article><article class="stack-group"><h3>Infra</h3><p>Commerce-ready deployment and caching patterns</p></article><article class="stack-group"><h3>Operations</h3><p>Merchant workflows, order tooling, promotion controls</p></article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Results &amp; Impact</span>
              <h2>Business outcomes supported by stronger engineering execution</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Metrics and operating improvements that made the product more effective and easier to scale.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stats-grid project-impact-metrics">
            <article class="stat-card"><strong>1.8x</strong><span>Faster product publishing workflows for internal teams</span></article><article class="stat-card"><strong>+22%</strong><span>Improvement in product page engagement after structure and UX refinements</span></article><article class="stat-card"><strong>-36%</strong><span>Catalog maintenance effort during campaign periods</span></article><article class="stat-card"><strong>99.9%</strong><span>Order processing reliability during peak traffic windows</span></article>
          </div>
          <article class="content-panel result-points">
            <ul>
              <li>Turned the store into a maintainable commerce system rather than a static storefront.</li><li>Improved conversion readiness with clearer checkout and catalog structures.</li><li>Reduced merchant overhead for catalog updates and order administration.</li><li>Created a stronger foundation for scaling campaigns, product lines, and integrations.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
